# Build context filter for agents/steward/Dockerfile.
#
# The context is the repo root (the Dockerfile says why), so this file decides
# what the hosted audit worker's image is allowed to see. Docker does NOT read
# .gitignore, so every private path has to be named here as well as there —
# `agents/steward/.env` is the one that matters most, because a `COPY
# agents/steward/` would otherwise bake three secrets into an image layer.

# --- Secrets. Never, under any circumstance. -------------------------------
**/.env
**/.env.*

# --- The private vault and working docs ------------------------------------
# Gitignored, present locally, and none of it is reachable from the worker.
# Copying it in would put a corpus of private prose inside a deployed container
# for no reason at all.
docs/
steward/
design_handoff_*/
.claude/
.agents/
.impeccable/
CLAUDE.md
AGENTS.md

# --- Never useful in an image ----------------------------------------------
node_modules/
**/node_modules/
.git/
.github/
dist/
.astro/
public/og/
agents/steward/.cache/
agents/steward/.temporal/
agents/steward/bin/

# --- The site. The worker audits it over HTTP; it does not build it. --------
# `src/`, `scripts/` and `tests/` at the root are the website's, not Steward's.
# Leaving them out is what makes "no site working copy at runtime" a property of
# the image rather than a promise about how the code behaves.
src/
scripts/
tests/
public/

# --- Steward's own bulk ----------------------------------------------------
# `reviews/` is a committed dataset, not code. The hosted worker registers
# neither `archiveReport` nor anything else that reads it — the scorecard's
# archive writes through the GitHub API now — so it is weight with no reader.
agents/steward/reviews/
agents/steward/vale/
